The route validates that `height` and `weight` are positive numbers, but there is no upper or lower bound check. A request with `height: 0.001` or `weight: 99999` passes validation and feeds into the BMI formula, producing nonsensical `estimateMeasurements` output that can cause `NaN` or `Infinity` to propagate into the DB or response.

The widget-side client validation (lines 1357–1370 in `size-chart.liquid`) correctly bounds the values, but the server must never trust client-side validation.

**Fix in `api.recommend.tsx` — after the `weight` check (after line 329):**
```tsx
// After metric/imperial unit check, add:
const isMetric = unit === "metric";
const minHeight = isMetric ? 100 : 40;
const maxHeight = isMetric ? 250 : 98;
const minWeight = isMetric ? 30 : 66;
const maxWeight = isMetric ? 300 : 660;

if (height < minHeight || height > maxHeight) {
  return Response.json(
    { error: `height must be between ${minHeight} and ${maxHeight} ${isMetric ? "cm" : "inches"}` },
    { status: 400, headers: CORS_HEADERS },
  );
}
if (weight < minWeight || weight > maxWeight) {
  return Response.json(
    { error: `weight must be between ${minWeight} and ${maxWeight} ${isMetric ? "kg" : "lbs"}` },
    { status: 400, headers: CORS_HEADERS },
  );
}
```

### ISSUE 5 — `api.analytics-event.tsx`: No `productId` or `sizeChartId` format validation — SQL/injection risk

**File:** `app/routes/api.analytics-event.tsx` lines 80–101

`productId` and `sizeChartId` are passed directly from the request body into `db.analyticsEvent.create` (line 116) without any format check. A malicious caller can stuff arbitrarily long strings (or special chars) into the analytics table. Prisma parameterises queries so SQL injection is not the issue, but an attacker can pollute the analytics DB with junk or cause index bloat.

**Fix — add after the `eventType` validation:**
```tsx
const PRODUCT_ID_RE = /^(\d{1,20}|gid:\/\/shopify\/Product\/\d{1,20})$/;
const CHART_ID_RE = /^[a-z0-9]{1,30}$/; // cuid format

if (productId !== undefined && productId !== null && !PRODUCT_ID_RE.test(productId)) {
  return Response.json({ error: "Invalid productId format" }, { status: 400, headers: CORS_HEADERS });
}
if (sizeChartId !== undefined && sizeChartId !== null && !CHART_ID_RE.test(sizeChartId)) {
  return Response.json({ error: "Invalid sizeChartId format" }, { status: 400, headers: CORS_HEADERS });
}
```

### ISSUE 6 — `api.analytics-event.tsx`: `metadata` object is unbounded — storage abuse vector

**File:** `app/routes/api.analytics-event.tsx` lines 107–110

The `metadata` field is JSON-serialised from the raw request body with no size cap. A caller can send a `metadata` object with megabytes of data and it will be written directly to the DB.

**Fix — clamp before serialising:**
```tsx
const metadataJson = (() => {
  if (metadata === undefined || metadata === null) return "{}";
  const serialised = JSON.stringify(metadata);
  if (serialised.length > 2048) return "{}"; // reject oversized metadata silently
  return serialised;
})();
```

### ISSUE 7 — `api.widget-config.tsx`: `customCss` field is returned raw to the storefront — XSS vector

**File:** `app/routes/api.widget-config.tsx` lines 103–104 and `extensions/size-chart-widget/blocks/size-chart.liquid` lines 1494–1498

The server returns `customCss` verbatim from the DB, and the widget JS inserts it into a `<style>` tag with only `</style>` stripped:

```javascript
// size-chart.liquid lines 1494-1498
styleTag.textContent = config.customCss.replace(/<\/style>/gi, '');
document.head.appendChild(styleTag);
```

`styleTag.textContent` is safe — it cannot inject script tags. However, CSS itself can contain `expression()` (old IE), `url()` with `javascript:` data URIs, or `@import` pointing to attacker-controlled stylesheets. This is a stored XSS / CSS injection risk if a malicious merchant (or someone with DB access) puts an `@import url(...)` in `customCss`.

Additionally, the `<\/style>` strip uses `textContent` assignment, so the `</style>` strip is redundant — `textContent` never interprets HTML. But the CSS content itself is unvalidated.

**Fix for `api.widget-config.tsx`:** Strip `@import` and `url(javascript:` from `customCss` before returning it, or simply omit the field from the public response and apply it server-side in the Liquid template instead. The safest fix:
```tsx
// Sanitise customCss before returning to storefront
const sanitisedCss = config.customCss
  ? config.customCss.replace(/@import\s/gi, '/* @import blocked */').replace(/url\s*\(\s*["']?\s*javascript:/gi, 'url(#')
  : "";
```

### ISSUE 8 — `shopify.extension.toml` is missing required fields

**File:** `extensions/size-chart-widget/shopify.extension.toml` (all 4 lines)

The TOML file has only `name`, `uid`, and `type`. It is missing the `handle` field (required for "Built for Shopify" badge verification), the `[ui_extension]` section is not needed for theme app extensions but the `[[blocks]]` array pointing at the liquid files is absent. More critically, Shopify CLI requires `extension_points` or equivalent target declarations. Without proper TOML structure the extension will fail `shopify app deploy`.

**Fix — replace the file entirely:**
```toml
name = "Size Chart Widget"
uid = "cb6a16f1-abd9-c9f7-6964-8e8c62e5f2878776186e"
type = "theme_app_extension"
handle = "size-chart-widget"

[settings]
  locales_path = "locales"
```

---

## MEDIUM SEVERITY ISSUES

### ISSUE 9 — Widget JS: `loadChart()` response is parsed incorrectly — chart never renders

**File:** `extensions/size-chart-widget/blocks/size-chart.liquid` lines 1207–1228

The API returns:
```json
{ "id": "...", "name": "...", "category": "...", "type": "...", "data": { "headers": [...], "units": {...}, "rows": [...] } }
```

At line 1221, the widget checks `resp.data.headers` — correct. But at line 1226:
```javascript
var data = Object.assign({ id: resp.id, name: resp.name, category: resp.category }, nested);
```
`nested` is `resp.data` (the inner object with `headers`, `units`, `rows`). After `Object.assign`, `data.headers` exists. But then `renderTable` is called with `data` and iterates `data.rows` using `data.headers` as column keys. The rows in the DB are keyed by the **header string** exactly (e.g., `"Chest"` not `"chest"`). If the header capitalisation changes, `row[h]` will return `undefined` silently. This is a latent bug, not broken today, but fragile.

More critically: at line 1201, `trackEvent('view', { sizeChartId: data.id })` is called, but `data.id` comes from `resp.id` — this is fine.

**Real bug:** The `loadInline()` function at line 1533 checks `!resp.data || !resp.data.headers` — correct. But `showTable()` at line 1181 re-fetches from `resp.data` without checking `resp.error` first. If the API returns `{ "error": "Chart not found" }` with a 404, `resp` is `null` (because `apiFetch` returns `null` for non-ok responses), so the `!resp` check at line 1209 catches it. This is correct. No bug here.

**Actual bug in `loadInline()`:** Line 1532 does `if (loading) loading.style.display = 'none';` but uses `style.display` (not the `hidden` attribute), inconsistent with all other show/hide code. This means if the loading spinner is ever re-shown by toggling the `hidden` attribute, the `style.display = 'none'` will override it and the spinner will stay hidden. Minor inconsistency.

### ISSUE 10 — `api.size-chart.tsx`: No OPTIONS handler for `GET` preflight on cross-origin requests

**File:** `app/routes/api.size-chart.tsx` line 58

The route handles `OPTIONS` in the `loader` function. In React Router 7, the framework may route `OPTIONS` requests to the `action` function (for non-GET methods) or the `loader` (for GET). Since this route has no `action` export, `OPTIONS` preflight requests will hit the loader — which is correct. This is fine as-is.

However, the `api.widget-config.tsx` route also only has a `loader` and handles `OPTIONS` there correctly. This is consistent.

### ISSUE 11 — Side-tab trigger: `position: fixed` is broken on iOS Safari with `overflow: hidden` on parent

**File:** `extensions/size-chart-widget/blocks/size-chart.liquid` lines 355–379 (`.scr-tab-trigger` CSS)

The side tab uses `position: fixed`. iOS Safari ignores `position: fixed` on elements inside a scrollable container or a container with `transform`, `filter`, or `will-change` applied. Many Shopify themes (e.g., Dawn) apply `transform: translateX()` to the main content during cart drawer open. When this happens, the tab trigger disappears or jumps to a wrong position.

**Fix:** The modal is already moved to `document.body` (line 1020). The same should be done for the side tab trigger. Move it to body via JS after creation when `style === 'tab'`:
```javascript
// in renderTrigger(), after the tab branch:
if (style === 'tab') {
  btn = document.createElement('button');
  // ...existing code...
  document.body.appendChild(btn); // append to body, not triggerWrap
  return; // don't append to triggerWrap
}
```

### ISSUE 12 — Modal close button: width/height 36px but min-height/min-width 44px — touch target visual mismatch

**File:** `extensions/size-chart-widget/blocks/size-chart.liquid` lines 458–466

```css
.scr-modal-close {
  width: 36px;
  height: 36px;
  min-height: 44px;
  min-width: 44px;
```

The element has `width: 36px; height: 36px` which overrides the `min-height`/`min-width` when inside a flex container with `flex-shrink: 0` set implicitly. The actual rendered size will be 36×36px, failing the 44×44px WCAG touch target requirement on iOS Safari.

**Fix:** Remove `width` and `height`, keep only `min-width` and `min-height`:
```css
.scr-modal-close {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  min-height: 44px;
  min-width: 44px;
  /* remove: width: 36px; height: 36px; */
```

### ISSUE 13 — Unit toggle buttons: `min-height: 32px` — fails 44px touch target requirement

**File:** `extensions/size-chart-widget/blocks/size-chart.liquid` lines 539–549

```css
.scr-unit-btn {
  min-height: 32px;
  min-width: 44px;
```

The `min-height` is 32px, below the 44px WCAG 2.1 AA touch target minimum. On iOS Safari, users with larger fingers will frequently mis-tap between "in" and "cm".

**Fix:** Change `min-height: 32px` to `min-height: 44px`.

### ISSUE 14 — `size-recommender.liquid`: radio label `min-height: 40px` — below 44px minimum

**File:** `extensions/size-chart-widget/blocks/size-recommender.liquid` line 301

```css
.scrr-radio-label {
  min-height: 40px;
```

**Fix:** Change to `min-height: 44px`.

### ISSUE 15 — `size-recommender.liquid`: redo button `min-height: 36px` — below 44px minimum

**File:** `extensions/size-chart-widget/blocks/size-recommender.liquid` line 469

```css
.scrr-redo {
  min-height: 36px;
```

**Fix:** Change to `min-height: 44px`.

### ISSUE 16 — `api.widget-config.tsx`: `Cache-Control: public` on a shop-specific resource

**File:** `app/routes/api.widget-config.tsx` lines 74–77

```tsx
"Cache-Control": `public, max-age=${CACHE_MAX_AGE}`,
```

`Cache-Control: public` tells CDNs and shared caches to cache the response. Since the URL is `?shop=xxx.myshopify.com`, a CDN could serve Shop A's config to Shop B if they share a cache key. Shopify's CDN is generally safe here since the path includes the query string, but Cloudflare or any proxy in front of the app server would need to vary on the full URL. Using `private` is safer since each shop's widget config is private data.

**Fix:**
```tsx
"Cache-Control": `private, max-age=${CACHE_MAX_AGE}`,
```

### ISSUE 17 — `size-chart.liquid`: `color-mix()` used without fallback — broken on iOS 15 and Samsung Internet 18

**File:** `extensions/size-chart-widget/blocks/size-chart.liquid` line 716 and `size-recommender.liquid` line 347

```css
box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--scr-primary) 20%, transparent);
```

`color-mix()` is supported from iOS 16.4 and Chrome 111. iOS 15 (still ~8% market share as of early 2026) and Samsung Internet 18 do not support it. The focus ring will be invisible on those browsers — a critical accessibility failure.

**Fix — add a fallback before the `color-mix` line:**
```css
.scr-input:focus,
.scr-select:focus {
  outline: none;
  border-color: var(--scr-primary);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15); /* fallback for older browsers */
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--scr-primary) 20%, transparent);
}
```

---

## LOW SEVERITY ISSUES

### ISSUE 18 — `size-chart.liquid`: `max-height: 92dvh` with `92vh` fallback order is wrong

**File:** `extensions/size-chart-widget/blocks/size-chart.liquid` lines 424–425

```css
max-height: 92dvh;
max-height: 92vh;
```

`dvh` (dynamic viewport height) is the modern unit that accounts for iOS Safari's retractable toolbar. The correct order for progressive enhancement is fallback first, override second:
```css
max-height: 92vh;     /* older browsers */
max-height: 92dvh;    /* modern browsers — overrides above */
```

Currently it's reversed: the `92dvh` line is written first, then overridden by `92vh` for all browsers. This means the `dvh` fix never applies and the modal will clip behind the iOS Safari bottom toolbar.

**Fix — swap the two lines** (line 424 and 425 of `size-chart.liquid`).

### ISSUE 19 — `size-recommender.liquid`: `scrr-powered-link` missing `aria-label` for screen readers

**File:** `extensions/size-chart-widget/blocks/size-recommender.liquid` lines 183–188

The footer link in `size-recommender.liquid` has no `aria-label`, unlike the equivalent link in `size-chart.liquid` (line 279 which has `aria-label="Size Chart & Recommender app"`). A screen reader will read the raw link text "Size Chart & Recommender" which is fine, but the `&amp;` entity will be read literally as "ampersand" in some screen readers.

**Fix:** Add `aria-label` to the link:
```html
<a
  href="https://apps.shopify.com/size-chart-recommender"
  class="scrr-powered-link"
  target="_blank"
  rel="noopener noreferrer"
  aria-label="Size Chart and Recommender app"
>Size Chart &amp; Recommender</a>
```

